{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019Ks3Fq4rJvCdK6C4oXEFDR","type":"tool_result","content":"00205932 WI-881: Checkpoint: SR-238, LLR-026 and TC-026 reconciled with the settled kill; narrow review 003 filed\n81695dd9 WI-881: The kill closes the job to new members before its snapshot, so no late member escapes the wait\n5f3d86b3 WI-881: The tree kill returns only once the session's group or job is empty, bounded at 10 s\n19dbc192 telemetry: session WI-881 verdict accepted\n36d3fe1a telemetry: session bc2c72de7af740c1b657f5454f1a396e ADJUDICATE COMPLETED\nc28b6a15 WI-881: Adjudicate sitting 001: amendment MEANING, not blessed; SR-238 returned\n5ddedc70 WI-881: Spine intent: a deadline or interruption ends the session's whole process tree\n19755fbf WI-881: A Windows session's deadline kill ends its tree through a job object, not taskkill\n24a3ff4f docs: WI-887 specref cites the split's decision record, not WI-884's moving spec\nf8e22961 pause: restored byte-identical after the scoped claims of WI-884, WI-828 and WI-831\n51ba46f0 claim: WI-831 -> active/wi-831 (bookkeeping)\n3f1e41f8 claim: WI-828 -> active/wi-828 (bookkeeping)\n?? docs/reviews/wi-881/004-ADJUDICATE-0020593.md\n?? docs/reviews/wi-881/004-ADJUDICATE-0020593.md.requested\n[decision.D-002]\ndecided = \"Answer sitting 001's first return by bounding the claim, not strengthening the mechanism: SR-238 and LLR-026 promise that every process still in the session's process group (POSIX) or job (Windows) ends. The residual is recorded as a decided gap in SR-238's Rationale: a POSIX descendant that leaves the group (setsid or setpgid), or a process another service starts on the session's behalf outside the job (WMI, the task scheduler, an already-running daemon), is not ended.\"\nalternative = \"Strengthen POSIX by tracking descendants instead of the group: a /proc walk on Linux plus a child subreaper, with no equivalent on macOS, or a cgroup, which needs privilege. Each is per-OS code for a descendant that detaches on purpose, and the Windows job has the analogous service-started residual anyway.\"\nreversal_cost = \"Medium: a later row adds descendant tracking behind _kill_tree and widens SR-238's acceptance; no interface changes.\"\nwhy_not_escalated = \"The sitting returned it as the coordinator's call. Bounding keeps the claim true to the mechanism, and the residual is written into the row where a reader finds it.\"\nreview = \"\"\n\n[decision.D-003]\ndecided = \"Answer sitting 001's second return by waiting, not softening: after the kill, _kill_tree returns only once the group (POSIX) or job (Windows, its active-process count) is empty, and raises if it is not empty within one stated bound of 10 s. SR-238's 'before the coordinator continues' then holds as written, the test asserts the grandchild is gone the moment run_session returns, with no grace period, and the stated bound is the overrun after which the coordinator stops with an error instead of continuing. Built (round 2): on Windows the job's active-process count reads zero a few milliseconds before a terminated process is signalled (the builder's measurement, 2026-10-10), so the wait also holds a SYNCHRONIZE handle on each job member taken just before the terminate and waits until each is signalled; the count alone left the interruption test red. Residual, recorded with D-002's: on POSIX a zombie still counts as a group member, so under a PID 1 that never reaps (a container run without an init) every deadline kill raises after the bound. That failure is loud, not silent; RESYNC_PACK tells adopters to run under a reaping init.\"\nalternative = \"Keep the kill asynchronous and state a grace bound in SR-238 (for example, descendants end within 5 s of the coordinator continuing), asserting that bound in the test.\"\nreversal_cost = \"Low: the wait is the tail of one function.\"\nwhy_not_escalated = \"The sitting offered both routes; waiting makes the stated threshold the code's behaviour rather than a tolerance.\"\nreview = \"\"\n\n[decision.D-004]\ndecided = \"Narrow review 002's F1 (a job member created between the handle snapshot and the terminate was never waited on; 12 of 12 trials) is answered by closing the job to new members before the snapshot: _kill_tree sets the job's active-process limit to 1, then snapshots, then terminates, so no process can join after the snapshot (the builder's measurements: 384 creations refused in the gap, 0 unsignalled at return; a refused creation runs no code and holds no inherited pipe). A live member the kill cannot open for SYNCHRONIZE (access denied, told apart from a gone PID by its error code) makes the kill raise after the terminate rather than be read as exited. The cost: a session whose descendants deny their own SYNCHRONIZE right errors at every deadline kill.\"\nalternative = \"Re-snapshot after the terminate (not airtight: after a job terminate a member leaves the PID list before it is signalled, 100 of 100), or a completion port (its messages are documented as not guaranteed). For an unopenable member, grant SYNCHRONIZE through the owner's WRITE_DAC right, which rewrites another process's permissions.\"\nreversal_cost = \"Low: inside _kill_tree's Windows half; no interface changes.\"\nwhy_not_escalated = \"It closes the reviewed class at its one owner without a fallback path; the unopenable-member cost is loud, and the DACL rewrite waits for a real case.\"\nreview = \"\"\n\n[decision.D-005]\ndecided = \"The kill drops a held handle whose process is no longer in the job (IsProcessInJob), so a reused PID never makes the wait hang on an unrelated process. Adjacent to F1, not part of it; built in the same round because it sits at the same boundary.\"\nalternative = \"Leave it for a later row.\"\nreversal_cost = \"Low: one check in the member wait.\"\nwhy_not_escalated = \"It removes a spurious wait without widening what the kill ends.\"\nreview = \"\"","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a511e73-e906-4589-9f31-ae73f8534ed7","uuid":"6675cb8e-5242-40ec-9fea-f3a443f2231c","timestamp":"2026-10-10T19:21:07.868Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"00205932 WI-881: Checkpoint: SR-238, LLR-026 and TC-026 reconciled with the settled kill; narrow review 003 filed\n81695dd9 WI-881: The kill closes the job to new members before its snapshot, so no late member escapes the wait\n5f3d86b3 WI-881: The tree kill returns only once the session's group or job is empty, bounded at 10 s\n19dbc192 telemetry: session WI-881 verdict accepted\n36d3fe1a telemetry: session bc2c72de7af740c1b657f5454f1a396e ADJUDICATE COMPLETED\nc28b6a15 WI-881: Adjudicate sitting 001: amendment MEANING, not blessed; SR-238 returned\n5ddedc70 WI-881: Spine intent: a deadline or interruption ends the session's whole process tree\n19755fbf WI-881: A Windows session's deadline kill ends its tree through a job object, not taskkill\n24a3ff4f docs: WI-887 specref cites the split's decision record, not WI-884's moving spec\nf8e22961 pause: restored byte-identical after the scoped claims of WI-884, WI-828 and WI-831\n51ba46f0 claim: WI-831 -> active/wi-831 (bookkeeping)\n3f1e41f8 claim: WI-828 -> active/wi-828 (bookkeeping)\n?? docs/reviews/wi-881/004-ADJUDICATE-0020593.md\n?? docs/reviews/wi-881/004-ADJUDICATE-0020593.md.requested\n[decision.D-002]\ndecided = \"Answer sitting 001's first return by bounding the claim, not strengthening the mechanism: SR-238 and LLR-026 promise that every process still in the session's process group (POSIX) or job (Windows) ends. The residual is recorded as a decided gap in SR-238's Rationale: a POSIX descendant that leaves the group (setsid or setpgid), or a process another service starts on the session's behalf outside the job (WMI, the task scheduler, an already-running daemon), is not ended.\"\nalternative = \"Strengthen POSIX by tracking descendants instead of the group: a /proc walk on Linux plus a child subreaper, with no equivalent on macOS, or a cgroup, which needs privilege. Each is per-OS code for a descendant that detaches on purpose, and the Windows job has the analogous service-started residual anyway.\"\nreversal_cost = \"Medium: a later row adds descendant tracking behind _kill_tree and widens SR-238's acceptance; no interface changes.\"\nwhy_not_escalated = \"The sitting returned it as the coordinator's call. Bounding keeps the claim true to the mechanism, and the residual is written into the row where a reader finds it.\"\nreview = \"\"\n\n[decision.D-003]\ndecided = \"Answer sitting 001's second return by waiting, not softening: after the kill, _kill_tree returns only once the group (POSIX) or job (Windows, its active-process count) is empty, and raises if it is not empty within one stated bound of 10 s. SR-238's 'before the coordinator continues' then holds as written, the test asserts the grandchild is gone the moment run_session returns, with no grace period, and the stated bound is the overrun after which the coordinator stops with an error instead of continuing. Built (round 2): on Windows the job's active-process count reads zero a few milliseconds before a terminated process is signalled (the builder's measurement, 2026-10-10), so the wait also holds a SYNCHRONIZE handle on each job member taken just before the terminate and waits until each is signalled; the count alone left the interruption test red. Residual, recorded with D-002's: on POSIX a zombie still counts as a group member, so under a PID 1 that never reaps (a container run without an init) every deadline kill raises after the bound. That failure is loud, not silent; RESYNC_PACK tells adopters to run under a reaping init.\"\nalternative = \"Keep the kill asynchronous and state a grace bound in SR-238 (for example, descendants end within 5 s of the coordinator continuing), asserting that bound in the test.\"\nreversal_cost = \"Low: the wait is the tail of one function.\"\nwhy_not_escalated = \"The sitting offered both routes; waiting makes the stated threshold the code's behaviour rather than a tolerance.\"\nreview = \"\"\n\n[decision.D-004]\ndecided = \"Narrow review 002's F1 (a job member created between the handle snapshot and the terminate was never waited on; 12 of 12 trials) is answered by closing the job to new members before the snapshot: _kill_tree sets the job's active-process limit to 1, then snapshots, then terminates, so no process can join after the snapshot (the builder's measurements: 384 creations refused in the gap, 0 unsignalled at return; a refused creation runs no code and holds no inherited pipe). A live member the kill cannot open for SYNCHRONIZE (access denied, told apart from a gone PID by its error code) makes the kill raise after the terminate rather than be read as exited. The cost: a session whose descendants deny their own SYNCHRONIZE right errors at every deadline kill.\"\nalternative = \"Re-snapshot after the terminate (not airtight: after a job terminate a member leaves the PID list before it is signalled, 100 of 100), or a completion port (its messages are documented as not guaranteed). For an unopenable member, grant SYNCHRONIZE through the owner's WRITE_DAC right, which rewrites another process's permissions.\"\nreversal_cost = \"Low: inside _kill_tree's Windows half; no interface changes.\"\nwhy_not_escalated = \"It closes the reviewed class at its one owner without a fallback path; the unopenable-member cost is loud, and the DACL rewrite waits for a real case.\"\nreview = \"\"\n\n[decision.D-005]\ndecided = \"The kill drops a held handle whose process is no longer in the job (IsProcessInJob), so a reused PID never makes the wait hang on an unrelated process. Adjacent to F1, not part of it; built in the same round because it sits at the same boundary.\"\nalternative = \"Leave it for a later row.\"\nreversal_cost = \"Low: one check in the member wait.\"\nwhy_not_escalated = \"It removes a spurious wait without widening what the kill ends.\"\nreview = \"\"","st